            "body": "> > What I have in mind is to check that `total_bytes = vim.api.nvim_buf_call(buf_id, function() return vim.fn.line2byte(vim.fn.line('$') + 1) end)` doesn't exceed certain value. Say 1000000 (1 Mb) or 100000 (100 Kb).\r\n> \r\n> Seems better, however shouldn't it be up to user to decide if he wants to enable syntax highlighting? You might want to disable it for specific languages etc. It doesn't have to do anything with treesitter being slow. Another thing to consider is hiding contents based on user defined conditions (could be .env files and so on). It would be nice to have a possibility to override `buffer_update_file`.\r\n\r\nIt feels like too much customization for my taste. I currently feel that having proper syntax highlighting (if it doesn't introduce visible overhead) is always better than having none.\r\n\r\nIf utilizing `MiniFilesBufferUpdate` works, then it should be fine.",
